      'UX fix: sync progress no longer blocks the user view. The full-window "First sync in progress" splash modal (v0.26.33) is gone. In its place, the sidebar footer\'s NetworkChip picks up the boot-sync state — when the app is doing its initial slice fetch, the chip shows "↓ 12.4 MB · first sync…" with a pulsing arrow and a brighter background. Same information, none of the window-blocking. User can navigate, open pages, even start typing into forms while data continues to arrive in the background; pages just render their empty state until their slice lands.',
      'Why the modal was a mistake: when I built it in v0.26.33 the goal was "user knows the app is alive" — but the cost was "user can\'t do anything for 5-30 seconds while we wait." The sidebar chip already conveyed the alive signal in steady state. Repurposing it to also show boot-state was the cleaner answer. Single canonical place for network status, no surface that pre-empts the user\'s attention.',
      'Sync semantics fix: remote catalog changes from other Macs now auto-apply silently instead of queuing behind a Refresh banner. Pre-v0.26.44 (since v0.22.2), every edit on another Mac surfaced a "N updates available — Refresh" pill at the bottom of the screen; user had to click it to merge. The pill itself was MORE intrusive than the auto-apply it was protecting against. Now: someone else edits a product → your Library list updates within ~250ms (debounced) → no banner, no click, no friction.',
