      'Phase 2 of 3 of the post-audit cleanup: converted the highest-impact sync FS calls in the main process to async. Specifically the protocol handlers (`app://` renderer assets + `app-image://` data assets) and the server\'s HTTP `serveAsset()`. These run on every image preview / asset request, and sync FS calls there could block the entire event loop while macOS waited on iCloud Drive to download a not-yet-local file — freezing HTTP, WebSocket, AND IPC at once.',
      'Three call sites converted: `protocol.js` `app://` handler (renderer assets), `protocol.js` `app-image://` handler (data folder), and `server/index.js` `serveAsset()` (HTTP asset serving to clients). All three now use `fs.promises.access` / `fs.promises.stat` via a small `fileExists()` helper instead of `fs.existsSync` / `fs.statSync`. The protocol handlers are inherently sync from Electron\'s side, but now only the single in-flight handler waits — not the whole event loop.',
      'Why iCloud was the real motivator: an iCloud-stored data folder (the v0.13.0 workflow for sharing data across two of your own Macs) has files that may need to download-on-access. A sync stat call against one of those files can block for seconds. Multiply by every product image preview in a Library grid render, every export thumbnail, every HTTP request a client makes — and the whole app freezes. The async conversion means each handler waits independently while the rest of the event loop keeps running.',
      'Tier B sync calls intentionally LEFT sync (with a brief justification in comments): the `mkdirSync` in `templateRenderer.outputPathFor()` and `processingPipeline` are inside Sharp pipelines that are already multi-hundred-ms operations; the per-call sync cost is negligible. The `existsSync` in `queueRunner`\'s filename-collision while-loop runs only 1-5 iterations per output file. The boot-path sync FS in `main/index.js`, `main/config.js`, `main/db/index.js`, and the migration helpers stay sync — those are one-shot startup operations where async would only add complexity.',
      'Tier C sync calls LEFT alone with no comment churn: `main/imageManager.js` (33 calls, almost all in one-shot batch-import / migration paths), `main/ipc/settings.js` (14 calls, user clicks Settings → rare), `main/ipc/files.js`, `main/fileHandler.js`. These run during user-driven actions that aren\'t shared with HTTP / WS / IPC streams. The full sweep would be valuable but the ROI is tiny vs the refactor risk. Audit punted them and the result is no functional improvement.',
      'Honest trade-off note: this isn\'t a "convert all 152 sync FS calls" release. It\'s a "convert the ones that actually block other concurrent work" release. The HTTP / protocol handlers are inherently shared resources; the rest are inside their own logical units. If a future profiler run shows the punted sites are bottlenecks, we revisit. Today they\'re fine.',
      'Tests: 38/38 pass. Both modified files syntax-check clean via direct `node -e require()` smoke test.',
